<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Nawawi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1233">1233</a>–<a href="/years/1277">1277</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Yahya ibn Sharaf al-Nawawi, Imam Nawawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yahya ibn Sharaf al-Nawawi was a jurist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> scholar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a>, born at Nawa near Daraa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and best known for The Meadows of the Righteous and his Sharh Sahih Muslim. He studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from the age of 18 under more than twenty teachers and settled there after his pilgrimage in 1253. In 1267 he became head of the Ashrafiyya Dār al-Ḥadīth in Damascus, a post he held until his death. Known for his asceticism, he never married, and he twice defied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> over taxes and endowments. He died at about 45, but his many works on hadith, theology and law are still read, and the Shafi'i school counts him and al-Rafi'i as the Two Shaykhs.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The last part of his name refers to his hometown, Nawa near Daraa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Biographical sources describe an unusually serious child. Yasin bin Yusuf Marakashi recalled seeing him at ten avoiding the games of other boys and busy with recitation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, and urged his teacher to take special care of him. His father then dedicated his life to the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> at eighteen and studied under more than twenty teachers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, jurisprudence, its principles and grammar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After making the pilgrimage in 1253 he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> as a private scholar. In 665 AH (1267 CE) he became head of the Ashrafiyya Dār al-Ḥadīth, one of the most prestigious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> institutions in the Islamic world, a post he held until his death. He ate one meal a day, paid no attention to clothing or comfort and never married, believing marriage would distract him from scholarship and worship. Al-Dhahabi described him as the master of the hadith scholars.</p><p>He twice drew the anger of the Mamluk Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> sought relief from heavy taxation after a years-long drought, he wrote that if Baybars did not stop taxing the residents abusively he would answer for it in the afterlife, and Baybars threatened to expel him. He replied that threats did not harm him and would not keep him from advising the ruler. In a popular tale he rebuked the sultan over a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waqf">waqf</a>, and Baybars yielded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 21 December 1277 and was buried the next morning. He is credited with at least fifty books, among them the Forty Hadiths, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> as-Saaliheen, a collection of hadith on ethics and conduct, the Minhaj al-Talibin, a manual of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> law, and a biographical dictionary. His Forty Hadiths and Riyadh as-Saaliheen made him respected in all legal schools, although he followed the Shafi'i. Admirers praised his scholarship, said to be more than forty pages a day from the age of eighteen. In 2015, during the Syrian Civil War, his tomb was demolished by rebels belonging to al-Nusra.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Nawawi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
